 ## Overview
-This family encompasses methods that reconstruct 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) scenes from uncalibrated or sparse image collections without relying on external Structure-from-Motion (SfM) pipelines for camera pose estimation [S1, 6136-instantsplat.md, 6137-anysplat.md, unofficial]. Unlike traditional 3DGS which requires dense pose estimation and iterative optimization [6136-instantsplat.md], these methods either predict primitives and poses directly via a feed-forward network [6137-anysplat.md, 6464-pixelsplat.md] or initialize Gaussians using geometry priors from foundation models to enable rapid, pose-free training [6136-instantsplat.md]. They solve the problem of local minima and computational cost inherent in sparse-view reconstruction by enabling real-time inference and eliminating the dependency on incremental depth priors or temporal continuity [6464-pixelsplat.md, 6888-joint-pose-optimization.md].
+This family reconstructs 3D Gaussian Splatting scenes from uncalibrated or sparse image collections without relying on external Structure-from-Motion pipelines [6136-instantsplat.md, 6137-anysplat.md, 6464-pixelsplat.md, 6888-joint-pose-optimization.md]. It sets itself apart from traditional 3DGS by eliminating iterative pose estimation, and from feed-forward alternatives by either initializing Gaussians via foundation model priors [6136-instantsplat.md] or jointly optimizing poses as learnable parameters [6888-joint-pose-optimization.md].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family rely on differentiable rendering to compute gradients from the rendered image to the Gaussian parameters (position, covariance, opacity, spherical harmonics) and/or network weights [S1, 6136-instantsplat.md, 6464-pixelsplat.md, unofficial]. The rendering process remains differentiable with respect to the scene representation [S1, 6136-instantsplat.md, 6464-pixelsplat.md, unofficial]. Loss functions typically combine photometric reconstruction error, such as $\mathcal{L}_{L1}$ or $\mathcal{L}_{MSE}$, with perceptual or structural metrics like $\mathcal{L}_{MS-SSIM}$ or $\mathcal{L}_{LPIPS}$ [6136-instantsplat.md][6464-pixelsplat.md].
+Builds on [Learning and Training Methods](9068-learning-and-training-methods.md).
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [InstantSplat](6136-instantsplat.md)
-InstantSplat is a pose-free, sparse-view 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) method that eliminates the need for Structure-from-Motion (SfM) pipelines by leveraging geometry priors from foundation models like MASt3R or VGGT. It enables training in seconds by directly initializing Gaussians from these priors, distinguishing itself from feed-forward methods like [pixelSplat](6464-pixelsplat.md) which regress primitives directly from images, and from traditional 3DGS which requires dense pose estimation.
+InstantSplat is a pose-free, sparse-view 3D Gaussian Splatting method that eliminates the need for Structure-from-Motion pipelines by leveraging geometry priors from foundation models like MASt3R or VGGT [6136-instantsplat.md]. It enables training in seconds by directly initializing Gaussians from these priors, distinguishing itself from feed-forward methods which regress primitives directly from images, and from traditional 3DGS which requires dense pose estimation [6136-instantsplat.md]. Choose this method when rapid reconstruction from very sparse views (3–10 images) is required without iterative optimization.
 
 ### [AnySplat](6137-anysplat.md)
-AnySplat is a pose-free, feed-forward network that reconstructs 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) scenes from uncalibrated image collections without requiring camera pose annotations. It differs from sibling methods like [InstantSplat](6136-instantsplat.md) and [pixelSplat](6464-pixelsplat.md) by handling arbitrary multi-view inputs (dense or sparse) in a single forward pass, yielding both scene primitives and per-image intrinsics/extrinsics simultaneously. This enables zero-shot evaluation on casual captures where pose data is unavailable, matching the quality of pose-aware baselines while maintaining real-time rendering latency.
+AnySplat is a pose-free feed-forward network that reconstructs 3D Gaussian Splatting scenes from uncalibrated image collections without requiring camera pose annotations [6137-anysplat.md]. It predicts 3D Gaussian primitives and per-image intrinsics and extrinsics in a single forward pass [6137-anysplat.md]. Unlike sibling methods, it scales to casual dense or sparse multi-view captures, matches pose-aware baselines, and surpasses prior pose-free methods in zero-shot evaluations at real-time rendering latency [6137-anysplat.md]. Choose this method when handling arbitrary multi-view captures where pose data is unavailable and real-time inference is needed.
 
 ### [pixelSplat](6464-pixelsplat.md)
-pixelSplat is a feed-forward model that learns to reconstruct 3D radiance fields parameterized by 3D Gaussian primitives from pairs of images, enabling real-time and memory-efficient rendering and fast 3D reconstruction at inference time. It solves the problem of local minima inherent to sparse and locally supported representations in generalizable novel view synthesis, where non-differentiable pruning/splitting (used in single-scene optimization) cannot be used because gradients must back-propagate through the representation. Unlike light-field transformers which do not reconstruct editable 3D scene representations, pixelSplat generates an explicit 3D representation that is renderable in real time, remains editable, and is cheap to train.
+pixelSplat is a feed-forward model that reconstructs a 3D radiance field parameterized by 3D Gaussian primitives from a pair of input images, enabling real-time and memory-efficient rendering [6464-pixelsplat.md]. It solves the problem of local minima inherent to sparse, locally supported representations in generalizable novel view synthesis, where non-differentiable pruning or splitting cannot be applied because gradients must back-propagate through the representation [6464-pixelsplat.md]. Unlike light-field transformers which produce black-box ray colors, pixelSplat generates an explicit, editable 3D representation [6464-pixelsplat.md]. Choose this method when working with stereo pairs and requiring an explicit, editable 3D representation with real-time rendering.
 
 ### [Joint Pose Optimization](6888-joint-pose-optimization.md)
-Joint Pose Optimization (JPO) eliminates the dependency on external Structure-from-Motion (SfM) pipelines by simultaneously optimizing camera poses and the scene representation (neural or Gaussian) from unposed images. Unlike sibling methods that may rely on incremental depth priors or temporal continuity, JPO directly minimizes photometric reconstruction error to solve for both geometry and pose. It is distinct from feed-forward methods that predict poses via a separate network, as JPO treats poses as learnable parameters optimized via gradient descent.
+Joint Pose Optimization refers to methods that simultaneously optimize camera poses and the scene representation to eliminate the dependency on pre-computed Structure-from-Motion pipelines [6888-joint-pose-optimization.md]. This approach relaxes the strict requirement for known camera parameters, allowing novel-view synthesis from unposed images using only photometric loss [6888-joint-pose-optimization.md]. It distinguishes itself from pose-free feed-forward methods by iteratively refining poses during training rather than predicting them in a single forward pass, though it typically requires rough initialization and is limited to small motions [6888-joint-pose-optimization.md]. Choose this method when iterative refinement is acceptable and camera motions are relatively small, avoiding the need for a trained feed-forward network.
 
 ### [Loss Scheduling for Candidate Head](13095-loss-scheduling-for-candidate-head.md)
-Loss scheduling for the candidate head is a training strategy that gradually reduces the influence of an image-dependent 'candidate head' ($\hat{C}_i$) during optimization, transitioning to a shared representation ($\hat{C}$) to stabilize pose estimation for hard-pose images. It prevents the shared representation from being distracted by wrong supervision introduced by hard-pose images in the early stages, allowing the model to assimilate these images into the shared representation once the shared model is accurate enough. This method is distinct from sibling methods like [InstantSplat](6136-instantsplat.md) and [pixelSplat](6464-pixelsplat.md) by explicitly decoupling the training influence of per-image heads via a cosine schedule rather than relying solely on joint pose optimization or fixed head structures.
+Loss scheduling for the candidate head is a training strategy that gradually reduces the influence of an image-dependent 'candidate head' ($\hat{C}_i$) during optimization, transitioning to a shared representation ($\hat{C}$) to stabilize pose estimation for hard-pose images [13095-loss-scheduling-for-candidate-head.md]. It prevents the shared representation from being distracted by wrong supervision introduced by hard-pose images in the early stages, allowing the model to assimilate these images into the shared representation once the shared model is accurate enough [13095-loss-scheduling-for-candidate-head.md]. This method is distinct from sibling methods by explicitly decoupling the training influence of per-image heads via a cosine schedule rather than relying solely on joint pose optimization or fixed head structures [13095-loss-scheduling-for-candidate-head.md]. Choose this method when training models with per-image candidate heads to stabilize pose estimation for challenging or hard-pose images.
 
